  "processNotes": {
    "state": "present",
    "source": "retro.json",
    "author": "implementer",
    "independent": false,
    "summary": "Repaired the service-desk workflow end to end and the follow-up lint regression: the service desk can assign named engineers, engineers are limited to assigned tickets, requesters alone confirm close or reopen, assignment/handling notices reach the in-app inbox, active tickets expose a 24-hour overdue state and service-desk reminders, Home is a role-aware queue/workload dashboard, New ticket accepts a screenshot, and fresh installations with APP_SAMPLE_DATA=true receive reproducible demo accounts and tickets. Follow-up repair exposed the inbox in the sidebar instead of a header button, so the shell no longer mounts React Query hooks outside the query provider. This round repaired the realtime entry path: the application now states its WebSocket endpoint instead of deriving it from the API URL, and the session-preparation boundary releases when a socket for the current session opens after the preparation timeout, so a missed open notification can no longer leave a working application behind an \"Unable to connect\" screen that Retry cannot clear. This round also proved the earlier \"Unable to connect\" report: the verification browser's domain containment replaces WebSocket with a wrapper that drops its static readyState constants, which made the realtime client believe it was connected and never dial; the client now restores those constants before startup.",
    "blockers": [],
    "improvements": [
      {
        "id": "notes/run/39502986334/attempt/1/I1",
        "category": "verification",
        "title": "Poll durable inbox delivery in integration tests",
        "detail": "NotificationService.send persists before asynchronous channel dispatch completes. A single inbox read directly after a transition can race that dispatch.",
        "suggestedChange": "Use a bounded poll of the real inbox endpoint when asserting user-visible notifications, as the ticket workflow test now does.",
        "mechanizable": true
      },
      {
        "id": "notes/run/39502986334/attempt/1/I2",
        "category": "verification",
        "title": "Cover the session-preparation boundary with an open that arrives after the timeout",
        "detail": "The barrier was only tested for ready, timeout and retry. The failure mode that blocked users was an open notification delivered after the provider unsubscribed, which no test produced.",
        "suggestedChange": "When touching an authentication or realtime gate, add a case that delivers the success notification after the gate's own timeout and asserts the gate releases.",
        "mechanizable": true
      },
      {
        "id": "notes/run/39502986334/attempt/1/I3",
        "category": "configuration",
        "title": "State derived client URLs explicitly in configuration tests",
        "detail": "api.realtimeURL was derived from api.baseURL through a `..` segment; the derivation worked only because the browser normalizes the path, so a proxy or CDN could break the handshake silently.",
        "suggestedChange": "Assert every derived-but-critical client URL (API, realtime, uploads) in a config test, and prefer stating it in client/config.",
        "mechanizable": true
      },
      {
        "id": "notes/run/39502986334/attempt/1/I4",
        "category": "verification",
        "title": "Exercise the client under browser instrumentation that wraps platform constructors",
        "detail": "A plain browser cannot see that containment wrappers may replace WebSocket without its static readyState constants, which made the application fail only in the verification browser.",
        "suggestedChange": "When verification wraps globals (allowed domains, init scripts, proxies), rerun the acceptance path through a test that stubs the wrapper, and treat missing static constants on any replaced constructor as expected environment behavior the application normalizes once at startup.",
        "mechanizable": true
      }
    ],
    "note": "实现者自述，未经独立核验；不是框架评审结论。"
  },
